# One canonical line ending, decided by the repository instead of by whoever
# checked it out.
#
# Before this rule the repo stored LF but a Windows checkout wrote CRLF, and
# only `core.autocrlf=true` in that one developer's global git config hid the
# difference. Any other tool reading the same working tree — WSL, a Linux
# container, an agent working through a mounted folder — saw all ~478 tracked
# files as modified, which is both useless noise and a standing invitation to
# commit a whole-file line-ending change by accident.
#
# It was not only cosmetic: `*.sh text eol=lf` below was added after the files
# were already on disk, and git never re-checks out a file just because an
# attribute changed. So 8 of 15 shell scripts stayed CRLF locally, and
# `bash deploy/server/test-preserve-chunks.sh` — a command RUNBOOK.md tells you
# to run — died on `set: pipefail: invalid option name`. That is the same
# failure that shipped install-health-guard.sh broken.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect and leave binary files alone; the explicit rules
# below remove any doubt for the formats this repo actually carries.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts run on Linux and MUST be LF in the repository. A CRLF file makes
# bash read `set -euo pipefail\r` and abort with "pipefail: invalid option name" —
# which is exactly how install-health-guard.sh got committed broken: an edit made
# from Windows wrote CRLF, and core.autocrlf=true did not normalise it back.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF (gradlew.bat).
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch the bytes.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.jar binary
*.wsz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
